[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says every date report is one issue, with one fix queued. Its own
cause table says otherwise, and so do the reports. There are three problems, each needing its own
fix, and each now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only the first problem. The other two keep coming in after it ships,
and they will be merged into a closed ticket.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## How to tell them apart

**parser:** the tenant writes the day first and the shared parser reads the month first, so
7 April comes out as 4 July. Any channel, any day-first tenant. This is what #4471 describes.

**hint:** the tenant runs booking-v3, whose date field shows the hint `MM/DD/YYYY`. People
type the month first, as the hint asks, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The
date comes out flipped too, but the fix is the hint, not the parser. These come in through the
form only, from booking-v3 tenants only. Intake tags them by the reporter's browser, so the
"tagged as" column on #4471 doesn't match: go by tenant and channel, as `causes.ts` does.

**one-day:** the date moves back one day, not across months: Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday
11 May; a meeting reaches Lisbon as Tuesday and Toronto as Monday. Nothing is flipped, so the
parser fix won't touch it. The desk had already noted these reports (`desk-notes.json`) but filed
them under the parser by tenant.

## Why they ended up together

Triage merges on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
and all three problems arrive under the same subjects: "Date is wrong", "Wrong day on the
invite". That page hasn't been reviewed since June 2024. Until a new decision replaces it,
check tenant and channel before merging a date report into a ticket.

